extraction_notes: "Rifts World Book 21: Splynn Dimensional Market printed 98-101 (cache p099-p102, page_offset 1; printed 99 is art). The Monster Were-Dragon opens in the right column of printed 98 with its Special R.C.C. Metamorphosis, whose bonus list runs into the left column of printed 100 with the penalties and the craving table; the shared stat block, headed Were-Dragon NPC Villain, Splugorth Slave or Optional Player Character, runs down printed 100 into the left column of printed 101. One block covers the ordinary and the Monster Were-Dragon; this class takes the Monster''s lines and the lines both share (ruling of 2026-11-18: the Monster is its own class). Printed 98, 100, 101 and 190 read off 170 dpi renders on 2026-11-19; every figure below matches the render. The text layer sets 1D6x10+62 as lD6x 10+62, M.D.C. and P.P.E. 3D4x10 as 3D4xlO and the money 3D4x100 as 3D4xlOO. || NAME: id monster-were-dragon (ruling of 2026-11-18). || ATTRIBUTES: the humanoid form''s, as printed for both kinds; the four percentage reductions are not applied to the dice (see PENALTIES). || POOLS: an M.D.C. being: 3D4x10 plus the P.E. attribute number in humanoid form, +1D6 per level. men_of_arms false: a race. P.P.E.: the block prints two lines for the Monster, P.P.E. 6D6+60 under the attributes and P.P.E. Base 3D4x10 plus the P.E. attribute number further down; the P.P.E. Base line is stored, and the 6D6+60 line is not, as the Were-Dragon class did. Horror Factor none in humanoid form. || XP: the block says Monsters use the Bio-Borg table, so xp_table copies production''s true-bio-borg ladder, read --remote 2026-11-19 (Nate''s decision 2026-09-26), and that ladder matches the render of Experience Table for Full Conversion Bio-Borgs on printed 190 level for level. || BONUSES: R.C.C. Bonuses in Humanoid Form, shared by both kinds: +2 initiative, +1 parry and dodge, +2 pull punch, +1 roll with punch as combat keys; +1 vs poison is toxins_poisons; +1 vs Horror Factor. They also apply in dragon form. || SECOND FORM: the monster dragon form is second_form (Monster Dragon Form; the class''s own block is Humanoid Form), carrying +10 P.S., the tail''s one additional melee attack, +2 save vs magic as spell_magic and ritual_magic, +2 vs possession, and Horror Factor 12. Stored as prose in special_abilities and not as a mechanic: the tripled M.D.C. (a second form''s pools take S.D.C. and hit points only), supernatural P.S. in dragon form, flight and its speed, the in-flight bonuses (conditional), the wing swipe and flying slash, the tail''s own strike, parry and dodge bonus and its damage, claws, bite, fire breath and its extra attack (once per melee only), and the +2 Horror Factor if mistaken for a real dragon. The ten dragon-form bullets are six special abilities and the second form. || PENALTIES: the five fixed penalties are in side_effects as prose. The four attribute reductions are percentages (I.Q. -30%, M.E. -20%, M.A. half, P.B. -30%) and a class bonus takes a number or dice, not a share of the roll; the alignment change is prose. || CRAVING: one rolled percentile table of six bands, stored as a pick-one group whose options are named for their bands (01-20, 21-40, 41-60, 61-80, 81-90, 91-00), each defined in special_abilities. The 91-00 band''s restoration of 1D4+2 M.A. and 1 I.Q. is that option''s attribute bonus; the 81-90 band''s no initiative bonus is prose. || SKILLS: base is the catalog base plus the printed bonus. Dragonese is a named row at 90, per_level 0. The native tongue is a pick from Language: Other at +40 (90%). Track (humanoids) is Tracking (people); Athletics (General) is Athletics (general). W.P. Staff or Blunt is a pick of one; W.P. One Ancient is a pick from the ancient W.P. rows; W.P. One Modern Weapon is a pick from the modern W.P. rows. || HAND TO HAND: Expert granted; Martial Arts costs one related pick, Assassin one and only if evil (the book''s one other skill selection). || RELATED (shared with the Were-Dragon, row 56, copied from production''s were-dragon): eight at level one with floors of two Physical and two Wilderness, and one at levels 2, 5, 8, 11 and 14, with the same category lines, except Weapon Proficiencies, which is any for Monster Were-Dragons. Secondary: four at level one and one at levels 3, 6, 9, 12 and 15 from the same list without the bonuses. || EQUIPMENT: catalog rows by slug, as the Were-Dragon''s, plus the Monster''s couple of heavy ancient-style weapons as a pick of two from the catalog''s heavy melee rows, the book naming none; which one is magical is the GM''s. || MONEY: player characters 3D4x100 credits; slaves none, a restriction line. || NOT STORED: size, weight, life span (half the ordinary 70-100 years), habitat, slave market value (800,000 to 1.5 million credits), allies and enemies are in the body. The alignment split (35% good, 30% selfish, 35% evil) is a restriction line, not a mechanic."
---

## Lore

The Were-Dragon is a slender humanoid that turns into a small winged serpent by a natural metamorphosis rather than by magic. For centuries the Minions of Splugorth have experimented on the race to learn how; the research gave them little, except that a series of painful Bio-Wizard treatments turns a Were-Dragon into a true creature of magic and a monstrous terror. About one third of all Were-Dragons have been subjected to the process.

Still mortal in humanoid form, the Monster Were-Dragon gains supernatural strength, Mega-Damage flesh and fire breath in dragon form. The treatments also dull its intelligence and make it irritable, aggressive, cruel and violent. Serene when left alone, once provoked it fights with merciless savagery, and the worst torture and kill for pleasure. Monster Were-Dragons are the rage of the arenas and prized as guardians and shocktroopers; many Conservators keep them as attack dogs, their loyalty enforced by mind-controlling symbiotes.

## Body

In humanoid form the same delicate, slender humanoid as an ordinary Were-Dragon, 5.5 to 6 feet (1.6 to 1.8 m) tall, but with eyes turned from blue to dark red. The monster dragon form is a serpent darker in color, bulkier and more robust than the ordinary dragon form, about 15 feet (4.6 m) long, half of it tail, with a thick dark hide, jagged points at the joints and spine, large wings lined with razor-sharp spikes and a thick tail made for striking. It weighs three times the humanoid form. Average life span is half the ordinary Were-Dragon''s 70-100 years, from the strain of the treatments.

## GM Notes

Habitat: prefers wilderness, particularly mountains and heavy forest, but can be found anywhere. Slave Market Value: manageable Monster Were-Dragons serve as guardians, attack animals, soldiers and arena combatants and command 800,000 to 1.5 million credits. Allies: attracted to other ruthless and powerful beings, half of them subservient to those of equal or greater power. Enemies: the Splugorth, their Minions, demons and other evil supernatural beings; even Monster Were-Dragons dislike true dragons. Only a couple dozen are believed to have escaped to Rifts Earth; four work among the Federation of Magic and two at Tolkeen. NPCs are level 1D4+1.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'monster-were-dragon');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'monster-were-dragon';

